    <string name="fix_prompt_instruction">Your last reply could not be read as a recipe: %1$s\nReturn only valid JSON matching this shape: %2$s\nReply with one JSON object and nothing else. Fix your last reply; do not add commentary.</string>
    <string name="fix_prompt_error_no_json">There is no JSON object in the reply.</string>
    <string name="fix_prompt_error_cut_off">The JSON is cut off: the object that opens at character %1$s never closes.</string>
    <string name="fix_prompt_error_not_valid">The JSON object is not valid JSON (parser message: %1$s).</string>
    <string name="fix_prompt_error_no_title">The \"title\" is missing or empty.</string>
    <string name="fix_prompt_error_no_ingredient">\"ingredients\" is missing or empty: the recipe needs at least one ingredient with a name.</string>
    <string name="fix_prompt_error_no_step">\"steps\" is missing or empty: the recipe needs at least one step with a body \"b\".</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_intro">You turn a recipe into JSON for the Simmer cooking app. Read the recipe below (or the photo or link I attach) and reply with one JSON object and nothing else: no commentary.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_rules">Rules:\n- \"category\" is one of Mains, Pasta, Breakfast, Sides, Soups, Salads, Desserts, Baking. \"tags\" is a subset of Vegetarian, Quick, Spicy, One-pot, Make-ahead. \"difficulty\" is Easy, Medium or Hard. \"icon\" is one of flame, chef-hat, utensils, egg, carrot.\n- Leave out any field you have nothing for (note, tips, if, d, needs, variations, methods). Every ingredient needs a name; every step needs a body \"b\".\n- \"time\" is total minutes and \"base\" is the number of servings the amounts are for (whole numbers; guess 4 if missing).\n- Amounts: \"q\" is a number; \"u\" is g, ml, tsp, tbsp, an empty string for countable items, or a [singular, plural] pair like [\"clove\", \"cloves\"]. Convert cups, ounces and pounds to g or ml.\n- Ingredient ids are i1, i2, ...; \"aisle\" is a short shop section such as Produce, Meat, Dairy, Bakery, Pantry or Spices.\n- Split the method into preparation steps (\"ph\": \"prep\", ids p1, p2, ...: chopping, mixing, anything without heat) and cooking steps (\"ph\": \"cook\", ids c1, c2, ...). Preparation comes first. Each step has a short title \"t\" and a body \"b\" of 1-3 sentences. In a body, mark each ingredient like this: {i1:chicken}.\n- \"d\" is the seconds a step needs to wait or cook (0 for none). \"needs\" lists the prep step ids a cook step uses.\n- Optional ingredients are variations of kind \"add\": give the ingredient \"if\": \"add:v1\" and the variation \"ing\": the ingredient\'s id.\n- \"methods\" are alternative ways to cook (oven or air fryer). Each option has \"v\", \"l\" (its label), \"icon\" (flame, zap, timer, layers or clock), \"time\" and \"d\". Mark the steps of each option with \"if\": \"m1=oven\"; steps of different options may share an id (c1 for the oven and c1 for the air fryer) because only one option is shown. The first option is the default. Otherwise use [].\n- Use °C and plain text, no emoji. \"notes\" lists anything you changed or could not read.\n- Write every text value in English. Keep the JSON keys, ids and the category, tag and icon words in English.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_enrichment">Make the recipe clearer:\n- Expand vague steps: heat levels, doneness cues, timings and technique.\n- Put tips about an ingredient in that ingredient\'s \"tips\". Write all other tips into the body of the step they belong to.\n- In \"notes\" add one short line per kind of addition (tips, suggestions, methods, restructuring).\n- The Kitchen rule below applies to everything you write, tips and added steps included.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_faithful">Stay faithful to the source: keep its amounts, ingredients and method. Keep the alternative methods it lists; do not add others and do not suggest extra ingredients. Put a side in \"sides\" only if the source itself lists it, as a self-contained side; do not invent sides.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_liberty">You may go beyond the source:\n- Suggest 3 to 5 extra ingredients that would improve the dish, as variations of kind \"suggest\" (not \"add\"). Each is an ingredient with \"if\": \"add:v2\" plus a variation whose \"d\" says what it adds to the dish, like \"smoky depth\".\n- Offer \"methods\" alternatives (oven, air fryer, pan, ...) whenever an appliance the cook owns fits, even if the source lists none. Never offer an appliance the cook does not own.\n- You may reorder, merge or split steps. Keep the amounts and the main ingredients.\n- If the recipe is only a main, offer up to 3 sides that go well with it in \"sides\". Each side is self-contained, with its own \"ingredients\" (about 6 at most), \"methods\" and \"steps\": 1 or 2 prep steps and 1 or 2 cook steps. Ids are local to the side. Give a side \"methods\" when it can be cooked another way (rice in a pot, a rice cooker or the microwave). Its \"d\" says why it fits. Sides are not mixed into the main\'s ingredients or steps, and there are no \"add:\" conditions in a side. If the recipe already lists its sides, keep those and add no more.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_schema_heading">Reply in this shape:</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_example_heading">Example of a finished reply (it shows the shape only; offer just the methods the Kitchen line below allows):</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_kitchen_any">Kitchen: the cook can use any appliance. Offer whichever methods suit the recipe.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_kitchen_only">Kitchen: the cook owns only these appliances: %1$s. Never offer a method option for an appliance that is not on this list. If the recipe needs one they do not have, adapt it to one they own and say so in notes.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_kitchen_none">none</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_kitchen_strict">Be strict: assume no other equipment beyond knives, a chopping board, bowls, spoons, a sieve, and pots and pans (only if Hob is listed). Rewrite any step that needs other tools, such as a grater, whisk, peeler or rolling pin, and say so in notes.</string>
    <string name="reader_prompt_kitchen_medium">Common small tools (grater, whisk, peeler, sieve, rolling pin, baking tray, measuring jug) are fine to assume; only adapt steps for larger appliances that are missing.</string>
